    <!-- ===================== SETTLE MEMORIAL ===================== -->
    <section id="settle-memorial">
      <h2 class="sec">Settle Memorial<span class="sub">Its Present Responsibility</span></h2>
      <hr class="rule2">

      <p class="lead">The building of Settle Memorial was started in 1906. The committee: Frank M. Thomas, John Carter, George W. S. Parrish, J. H. Hickman, Frank Katterjohn, W. E. Parrish, D. C. Stimson, M. L. Ogden, R. S. Hughes, John Wright, J. A. Harris, J. N. Grady. Of these men, one, Mr. Harris, has moved, and only three are now living: Frank Katterjohn, D. C. Stimson and M. L. Ogden.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">15</span>Mr. John P. Davis superintended the building. The cost was about $75,000. It is strictly English Gothic in style, inspired by sincere Christian conviction. It is really the embodiment of Christianity. Its characteristics are the high pointed arches, vaulted ceiling, predominating vertical lines, symbolic decorations, stained glass windows, columns with foliage capitals, color and gilding, and wrought iron hinges. Every part of the decorations is charged with meaning: the Cross, symbol of Christ's Cross; the inverted cross, that the supreme sacrifice had been made; the trefoil stands for the Trinity; the quatrefoil, the four gospel writers; the circle, eternity; the square, the new Jerusalem, the city foursquare; the laurel leaf, wisdom; the olive leaf, peace; the dove, the Holy Ghost; the vine, &ldquo;I am the Vine&rdquo;; the seven-branched candlestick, the seven churches established by Paul. In the front window the angel with the sword represents the church militant; the angel on the right, the church triumphant. The predominating vertical lines and high pointed arches give one a feeling of awe and lift their thoughts to God, as also the ceiling in soft blue and white fleecy clouds, reminding one of the heavenly skies. The radiant beauty of the stained glass windows, with their gorgeous coloring and hand-painted heads, adds to the effect. These are the only hand-painted windows in the city and the most artistic. They were painted and made by Mr. Louis Millet of Chicago, the president of the Art Institute of Chicago, and nephew of the great French artist Millet, the noted painter of peasants. The window committee was Dr. Frank M. Thomas, Mr. and Mrs. R. S. Hughes, and Miss Cora Lee Webb. The wall decorations are exquisitely beautiful, frescoed in soft tan, blue, red and green, and with gold trimmings on the organ arch. The hand carving is simple but appropriate, carrying out the Gothic ideas. The gold candlesticks were given by Miss Shumaker in memory of Elizabeth and Thomas Thomas, and their names are engraved on them. Dr. Thomas gave the Bible in memory of his son Frank, Jr. Miss Jennie McClarty gave the individual communion service.</p>

      <p>The church is built of stone and white brick. The foundation stone &mdash; broad, strong, good, like the doctrine of Methodism &mdash; is deeply embedded in the earth. In the corner stone was placed some appropriate and interesting articles, among them a paper containing the news of Dr. Spurrier's death. This was brought by Mrs. Rose Parrish.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">16</span>Every brick and stone was placed with care and watched by Dr. Thomas, that nothing of inferior quality went into its building. Quite a calamity occurred during the erection of this church. One of our finest young men, Mr. Claude Basham, lost his life while at work on it. Having charge of the stone work, he was most careful that everything should be just right. One day, while working on the eastern tower, he slipped and fell to his death. A gloom was cast over the entire church, for he was a young man of sterling qualities &mdash; upright, honest, a God-fearing Christian &mdash; and his untimely death was mourned by many friends. A marble memorial slab for him is on the wall of the eastern vestibule.</p>

      <p>After the formal opening of the church, all the women's societies were disbanded and every woman in the church was placed in one of the ten bands organized. Each band had a president and other officers, and Mrs. R. W. Littell was president of all the bands. These band presidents were Mrs. Judith Duke, Mrs. M. Eskridge, Mrs. George R. Hayes, Mrs. Frank Katterjohn, Mrs. Steward, Mrs. Robert Todd, Mrs. H. A. Moore, Mrs. N. B. Chambers, Mrs. Will Deane, and Miss May Read.</p>

      <p>The church was now $33,000 in debt. These bands went to work to help pay the debt, and in the fall when conference came, two bands had charge of serving the meals for a day. It was most satisfactory, and the ladies enjoyed it very much.</p>

      <p>The next pastor, Dr. J. H. Young, 1908&ndash;1910, was a transfer from St. Louis. Dr. Young was a handsome, dignified, cultivated man, dearly loved by those who really knew him and his intrinsic worth.</p>

      <p>Dr. Young was succeeded by Dr. Lewis Powell, 1910&ndash;1914, a transfer from Mississippi. Dr. Powell was a real man, a strong Christian character &mdash; practical, genuine, highly educated, a most efficient executive, true as steel, to whom a lie was a lie and a thief a thief. He was no respecter of persons and could not be influenced by social standing or by wealth. One of his neighbors, who was not noted for his religious tendencies, told him that when he died, no matter where Dr. Powell might be at the time, he must come and preach his funeral, for he was the only minister he knew who would tell the truth about him. These sterling qualities, with his educational advantages and his true religious fervor, placed him in a class to himself. He was admired and respected by all. Dr. Powell walked into that debt, and into the members as well, telling them that anyone in debt and making no effort to meet their obligations was both a liar and a thief.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">17</span>The members seemed to like being told the plain truth, for that first year $27,000 was paid in, $19,000 being applied to the debt. Settle Memorial owes much to Dr. Powell, who had the stamina to tell them the truth and make them do their duty.</p>

      <p>Then came Dr. J. R. Savage, 1914&ndash;1916, a transfer from the Kentucky conference, to which he was returned at the end of the second year. His short stay endeared him and his charming wife to many, both in and out of the church.</p>

      <p>The next pastor was the saintly Sam Miller, 1916&ndash;1918, the finest, most spiritual character of all Settle Memorial ministers. If he possessed a single fault, no one ever discovered it; gentle, sweet, appreciative of every favor, yet strong and efficient in all his undertakings, he was beloved by all.</p>

      <p>During the pastorate of Dr. Miller, the debt on Settle Memorial was paid in full, and joy filled every heart. Jubilee week was held April 30&ndash;May 6, 1917, to which all the former pastors were invited to come and participate. The program was as follows:</p>

      <div class="roster">
        <dl class="program">
          <div><dt>Monday, April 30</dt><dd>Dr. Sam Miller &mdash; &ldquo;A Voice From The Heights.&rdquo;</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Tuesday, May 1</dt><dd>Dr. J. R. Savage &mdash; &ldquo;The Unerring Guide.&rdquo;</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Wednesday, May 2</dt><dd>Dr. J. H. Young &mdash; &ldquo;More Abundant Life.&rdquo;</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Thursday, May 3</dt><dd>Dr. Lewis Powell &mdash; &ldquo;Some of the Fundamentals.&rdquo;</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Friday, May 4</dt><dd>Dr. Frank Thomas &mdash; &ldquo;The Shadow of a Man.&rdquo;</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Sunday, May 6</dt><dd>Bishop McCoy, Birmingham, Ala.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Sunday, May 6, 3 p.m.</dt><dd>Settle Memorial Methodist Episcopal Church, South was dedicated by the Bishop.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <p>During the flu epidemic in 1918, Dr. Miller contracted the dreadful plague and sank under its treacherous course. His death cast a gloom not only over the church, but the entire community, as well as the conference, whose secretary he was. The church was downcast and discouraged, feeling there was no one who could take his place. But it was only another proof that &ldquo;God moves in a mysterious way his wonders to perform.&rdquo;</p>

      <p><span class="folio">18</span>For now there came to us the brilliant, talented, companionable, charming Carl C. Gregory and his adorable wife, 1918&ndash;1923. He was young, this being his first real charge, having been the assistant of Dr. George Stuart of Birmingham. He was unafraid, but the board was not. They looked at his young, boyish face and their hearts sank. The chairman of the board met him at the train, and on the way to the hotel said, &ldquo;This is a mighty big church,&rdquo; and Mr. Gregory replied, &ldquo;Yes, we have big churches in Birmingham too.&rdquo; After the first Sunday they felt better, and soon realized they had nothing to fear and much for which to be thankful.</p>

      <p>One day Mr. Gregory said to the janitor, &ldquo;Henry, see that the balcony is in good condition; we are going to fill it.&rdquo; Henry replied, &ldquo;Mr. Gregory, that has never been did; it cannot be did.&rdquo; Mr. Gregory said, &ldquo;Henry, it is going to be did.&rdquo; And it was. As the years slipped away, even the members who firmly believed in the Methodist system of four years were only too delighted to keep him over the prescribed time. When the Bishop thought best to transfer him to Texas, where he went to take charge of one of the wealthiest churches in the South, Settle Memorial was heartbroken, but very proud that he had accomplished so much. As they bade adieu to him, his lovely wife and charming children, their prayers and blessings went with them. Their return visits were looked forward to with great anticipation, and the rejoicing at their return was like that over the Prodigal Son. Settle Memorial always felt they belonged to them, and when they came it was simply a homecoming. His unfortunate death from overwork was a shock and a calamity, not only to the church but to all Methodists. It was a time to remember &mdash;</p>

      <p class="verse">&ldquo;God's in His heaven,<br>All's right with the world.&rdquo;</p>

      <p>B. G. Hodge, 1923&ndash;29, another transfer from the South, and again from Birmingham, and Dr. George Stuart's assistant. Succeeding the popular Carl Gregory, he had a hard task before him; but he was young, ambitious, and was noted for his charming personality, consequently he had no trouble in winning his way into the hearts of his members.</p>

      <p>Just now the question was stirred in regard to an educational building. This movement was opposed by some of the more conservative business members. They felt it was not the proper time. Their objections were overruled and the building erected at a cost of $54,000. The building committee: D. C. Stimson, W. R. Jagoe, E. W. Smith, F. E. Katterjohn and O. L. Fowler. The finance committee: T. L. Barr, D. J. Glenn, Earl Felix, and A. C. Rose. The furnishing committee: J. W. Snyder, H. H. Smith, D. W. Kendall, Mrs. Gertrude MaQuigg, and Dr. W. A. Wheeler. It was soon discovered the conservatives were right. Very few funds had been promised &mdash; not enough to even pay the interest on the borrowed money. Then the depression came, and for five years no effort was made to pay even a small part of this indebtedness. This was inexcusable for a church of Settle Memorial's standing, which had always met its obligations. Now the debt had grown to $65,000, and the church has awakened to the enormity of her carelessness and indifference.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">19</span>In 1928 the Kilgan organ was bought and installed at a cost of $14,000.</p>

      <p>In 1925, Settle Memorial again entertained the Louisville conference.</p>

      <p>Dr. Lyon, 1929&ndash;1930, one of the best educated and most capable men in the conference, was the next pastor. He was practical-minded, had many sterling qualities, and no doubt would have been of great benefit to the church had he been given the opportunity, but he remained only one year.</p>

      <p>Settle Memorial's next minister was another transfer, this time from St. Louis: Dawson C. Bryan, 1930&ndash;1934. Unfortunately, Mr. Bryan was pastor during the depression, and consequently very little could be accomplished; but in spite of this, all budget obligations were paid in full, and in 1932 the church again entertained the Louisville conference.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">20</span>In the fall of 1934 the Bishop sent to this charge Mr. Bedford Turner. Though he has been the pastor for only a few months, he has taken charge of the difficulties with a firm hand. He has already gained the confidence of his members. By his many sterling qualities, his executive ability, sincerity and hopeful outlook, he has imbued them with confidence in his leadership and with courage to go forward and rid the church of its indebtedness, and they will. The spirit of their Methodist forefathers is pushing them forward. They will live up to their heritage.</p>

      <p>On Easter Sunday, April 21, 1935, at the request of the pastor, a cash offering was made to be applied on the interest of the church debt, which was now $10,340. Three thousand dollars was asked for, and $5,480 was brought and laid on the altar. This, with a contribution from the bondholders, enabled the finance committee to clear the interest on this bonded indebtedness. It was a joyous occasion, and added new life and encouraged the congregation to renewed efforts to cancel the entire debt in the near future.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">21</span>There have been several persons serving as assistant to the pastor, namely: Misses Rosa Lowe, Len Bailey, Ruth Baker, Alma Pitts, Annie Vick, Mrs. Maude Fullerton, Miss Christopher and Mrs. Gregson. These ladies have served faithfully, and their reports to the board should make the board ashamed of their own records.</p>

      <p>Mrs. Maude Fullerton was one of the most popular women in the church. Though her stay here was short, she endeared herself to all who had the pleasure of knowing her. A beautiful character, a lovely woman with a charming personality, cheerful even in the face of greatest pain, she never bored others with her troubles, and few knew her condition. Her death, after rallying from an operation, came as a great shock to her scores of friends and admirers, especially to the young people of the church with whom she worked and who adored her.</p>

      <p>There have been many revivals in Settle Memorial, and two stand out above the others. One was conducted by a little blind man brought here by Dr. Powell, who had known him a long time. This afflicted man radiated Christianity; and looking at him, one realized their own unworthiness, ungratefulness and selfishness. One night he told the story of his going blind, and it seemed as though God himself was right in the midst of them. There was an awe over the entire congregation, and they passed out of the church without a sound or whisper.</p>

      <p>Another great meeting was one conducted by Bishop Darlington. But who could do him justice? Those who know him, or have come under the spell of his preaching, feel that it is a benediction just to be in his presence and look on his face, which so plainly reflects his character, and to hear his glorious voice singing &ldquo;Pass me not, O gentle Savior.&rdquo;</p>

      <p>When one takes a backward glance to 1907 and thinks of the men and women who gave, worked and sacrificed in order to help make Settle Memorial the beautiful structure it is, face after face rises up to greet us. Some have passed to their reward; others remain and are still striving to make God's work go forward. Some of these who have crossed the river, and are worthy of special notice, are T. H. Guthrie, W. B. Cosby, W. B. Palmer, R. S. Hughes, W. E. Parrish, George W. S. Parrish, N. B. Chambers, L. P. Little, John Wright, John Carter, Otis Parrish, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Hoppe, El Duke and Alexander Hill. Some are still living: E. W. Smith, D. C. Stimson, W. R. Jagoe, B. D. Ringo, Frank Katterjohn, A. B. Thompson, H. H. Smith, Mrs. Will Parrish, Mrs. R. W. Littell, Mrs. D. C. Stimson, Mrs. H. A. Moore, Mrs. N. B. Cooke, Miss May Read, Mrs. Frank Katterjohn, Mrs. M. Eskridge, Mrs. R. S. Hughes, and many others too numerous to mention.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">22</span>Mr. R. S. Hughes was ever Dr. Thomas' right-hand man. Always interested in everything pertaining to the church, which he loved, he gave freely of his time and means. During the erection of the church he was on the grounds almost daily, seeing that everything was as it should be, helping to disentangle the many difficulties which arose, and serving where needed. His death was a great loss to the church.</p>

      <p>This list would not be complete without some mention of our sexton, Henry Edwards. Henry was employed in 1894, but for some cause he gave up the position for two or three years, when he was re-employed and has continued in the service ever since &mdash; about thirty-eight years in all. Henry is a good Baptist, but he belongs to us, and we belong to him. All through these years he has been prompt, faithful, honest, and could be trusted with anything and anywhere. He has ever looked after the interest of his &ldquo;folks&rdquo; (as he calls them), tried to make them comfortable, and to please them. When special collections were taken, Henry was right there with his offering, often giving more than he could afford. It will be hard to find a truer, more faithful servant than Henry has been; those who have worked with him and know him thoroughly love him for his fine sterling qualities and Christian character, and feel sure his reward in the hereafter will be commensurate with his services in the House of the Lord.</p>

      <p>The choir has ever been an added attraction to the service, the organists real musicians. They have been Miss Agnes Osborne, Miss Ella Goodson, Miss Margaret Keeley, Prof. L. Quigley, Miss Cassatt, Mrs. W. H. Horner, Mrs. Katherine Cassidy, Miss Margaret Franks, Miss Louise Jones and Mrs. Gene Mobberly.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">23</span>Among those who for years gave their voices free, feeling that God had given them this talent and it was only just and right to use it freely for Him, are Mrs. M. L. Ogden, Mrs. Yewell Bottorf, Mr. and Mrs. Frank E. Katterjohn, and Mr. E. W. Smith.</p>

      <p>Mrs. Ogden for forty years sang in the choir, and was chairman of the music committee twenty-five years, and for thirty-five years played the piano for prayer meeting.</p>

      <p class="role"><b>Present choir:</b> Mrs. Gene Mobberly, organist; Miss Olga Leaman, director; Mrs. Yewell Bottorf, Mrs. T. R. Bannister, Mrs. S. A. Burns, Mrs. John Fletcher Barr, Mrs. George Bailey, Mary Jewell Mitchell, Miss Jeanne Davis, Miss Barbara Mitchell, Miss Rosa Bailey, Messrs. Daryl Bogard, C. O. Sandefer, Ray Purdy, Hewlett Whitmer, Walter Ellis, and Dr. Foster.</p>

      <p>The Woman's Missionary Society was organized in 1881, both the foreign and home. They united in 1915. The presidents of the Foreign Missionary Society were Mrs. W. D. Stirman, Mrs. M. S. Drabelle, Mrs. Snyder, Mrs. T. H. Frayser, Mrs. T. R. Kendall, Mrs. M. Eskridge and Mrs. H. A. Moore.</p>

      <p>The presidents of the Home Missionary Society were Mrs. Robert McJohnston, Mrs. N. B. Cooke, Mrs. G. A. Park and Mrs. R. S. Hughes.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">24</span>These societies have many firm believers in their cause, and many who give most of their time to increase the interest. Mrs. N. B. Cooke has from its beginning been an interested worker along this line. Also Miss May Read, Miss Vi Jones and Mrs. R. S. Hughes. They have remained staunch, true workers; their interest has never flagged. Mrs. H. A. Moore, another faithful adherent, has been first, last and always a missionary advocate. She was the society's president for nineteen years, and for twenty-two years never missed a meeting. These women, though thoroughly believing it right and proper to devote their time to this cause, did not for this reason refuse to do their duty elsewhere, but have always responded willingly when called upon.</p>

      <p class="role"><b>The Missionary Society</b> now has enrolled 200 members: Mrs. W. L. Fulton, president; Mrs. H. M. Berry, first vice president; Mrs. Burns, second vice president; Mrs. Brewer Montague, secretary; and Mrs. D. W. Kendall, treasurer. Band leaders: Mrs. J. W. Ament, Mrs. H. B. Warner, Mrs. Frank Roberts, Mrs. O. L. Fowler, Miss Pearl Read and Miss Vista Morris.</p>

      <p>The Missionary Society has two associate societies: &ldquo;The Business Women,&rdquo; which meets in the evening, and &ldquo;The Pollyannas,&rdquo; a society of young people.</p>

      <p class="role"><b>The Business Women:</b> Miss Pearl Read, chairman; Miss Anna Carr Bennett, secretary; Mrs. Fleming Bowlds, treasurer.</p>

      <p class="role"><b>The Pollyannas:</b> Miss Vista Morris, chairman; Mrs. Martha Pollard, first vice president; Mrs. Mary Rose Barr, second vice president; Miss Louisa Ellis, secretary; Miss May Cromwell, treasurer.</p>

      <p>The Methodists have entertained the conference eight times in its history: in the first church in 1853, 1862 and 1869; in Settle Chapel in 1881 and 1894; in Settle Memorial in 1908, 1925 and 1932. They have lost three ministers by death: the first church, J. C. Berry; Settle Chapel, Dr. Spurrier; and Settle Memorial, Dr. Miller.</p>

      <p>The Sunday School, the strongest institution of the church, is in a flourishing condition. It has been served by many worthy men as its superintendent. Their names: W. E. Parrish, Frank Katterjohn (serving three times), Ernest Arnold, James H. Risley and J. W. Snyder. Enrollment at present, 542. It is well equipped for the work &mdash; a splendid building, eight pianos and necessary supplies, an excellent cohort of teachers and officers.</p>

      <div class="roster">
        <p class="rtitle">The present officers and teachers</p>
        <p class="role">J. W. Snyder, Superintendent; J. Eugene Gilbert, Secretary; Helen Hill, Treasurer; Mrs. W. N. Gore, Information Dept.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Nursery Department</b> &mdash; Mrs. M. E. Rushing, Superintendent. Teachers: Mrs. Jay O'Flynn, Mrs. Rolla Taylor, Mrs. Jodie Duvall, Mrs. Parker O'Brien.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Beginners Department</b> &mdash; Mrs. G. Newton Parrish, Supt.; Mrs. H. M. Berry, Secretary; Mrs. Lee Truman, Teacher.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Primary Department</b><span class="folio">25</span> &mdash; Mrs. F. E. Katterjohn, Supt.; Mrs. George Bailey, Assistant Supt.; Mrs. D. W. Kendall, Secretary; Mrs. Zeno Miller, Pianist. Teachers: Mrs. W. H. Strother, Mrs. H. H. Smith, Mrs. W. J. Martin, Mrs. J. W. Barr, Mrs. A. R. Renfrow, Mrs. J. W. Green, Jr., Mrs. W. A. Lashbrook, Mrs. G. W. Hall.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Junior Department</b> &mdash; John Hills, Superintendent; Louisa Ellis, Secretary; Mrs. J. F. Barr, Pianist. Teachers: Mrs. John Hills, Miss Anna Ford McClanahan, Miss Viola Morris, Mrs. Bedford Turner, John C. Kessler.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Intermediate Department</b> &mdash; J. E. Darnell, Superintendent. Teachers: Mrs. J. F. Walker, Miss Sarah Thigpen, Talmage Hocker.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Senior Department</b> &mdash; Mrs. J. H. McKinney, Superintendent. Teachers: Mrs. Mollie Webb Irwin, Miss Anna Carr Bennett, F. W. Rickard, George Smith, Brown Thigpen.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Young People Department</b> &mdash; Mr. H. H. Smith, Superintendent; Mrs. W. L. Woolfolk, Teacher. Officers: Mrs. Luther Luckett, Vice President; Miss Katherine Coots, Secretary-Treasurer.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Adult Department</b> &mdash; Mrs. Yewell Bottorf, Superintendent; Mrs. John Cox, Asst. Supt.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Susannah Wesley Class</b> &mdash; Teachers: Miss Cora Lee Webb, Mrs. Gertrude McQuigg. Officers, 1934&ndash;1935: Mrs. Velva Bowers, President; Mrs. Fleming Bowlds, First Vice President; Miss Mary Lancaster, Second Vice President; Mrs. Julia Gist, Treasurer; Miss Ethel Mason, Assistant; Mrs. Pearl Swatzenburg, Class Treasurer; Miss Lucy Barr, Fellowship Chairman; Mrs. E. C. Young, Pianist; Miss Olga Leaman, Song Leader. Band Leaders: Mrs. Junius Eads, Mrs. Albro Duncan, Mrs. George Myles, Mrs. G. R. Mason.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>E. W. Smith Class</b> &mdash; Mr. E. W. Smith, Teacher; Mrs. L. E. Morris, President; Mrs. M. L. Ogden, Vice President; Mrs. Melville Eskridge, Secretary; Mrs. Bryan, Treasurer.</p>

        <p class="role"><b>Sam Miller Bible Class</b> &mdash; Judge A. D. Kirk, Teacher; F. E. Katterjohn, Assistant Teacher; Mr. O. H. Christie, President; W. L. Fulton, Vice President; Allan Roberts, Secretary-Treasurer; C. W. Cromwell, Assistant; Mrs. M. A. Stimson, Pianist; Mrs. Yewell Bottorf, Song Leader.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Some members enlisted on its roll as teachers on account of their faithfulness and loyalty for so many years are worthy of special mention. Mrs. Mary Ellen Hart, who for more than thirty years cared for the little tots. Later on she was assisted by her capable daughter, Mrs. E. W. Smith. Many men and women today recall with pleasure the Sunday morning class of long ago, when they sat at the feet of Mrs. Hart, listening to her stories, singing &ldquo;Little Jewels,&rdquo; or having the honor of sitting in the birthday chair. The happy hours spent there, her unfailing kindness and sweet smile will always bring delightful recollections to all who were fortunate enough to have come under her influence.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">26</span>Another earnest, reliable teacher was and is Mrs. Frank Katterjohn, who has served not only here, but everywhere in the church where work was to be done, for nearly forty years. Hundreds of the primary children adored &ldquo;Miss Mamie&rdquo; and look upon her as their second mother.</p>

      <p>Mr. E. W. Smith, since his coming to Owensboro more than forty years ago, when he identified himself with the church, has ever been one of its most loyal, substantial supporters. Well educated, a conservative business man, ever on the right side of any question, he has served in every capacity &mdash; on the board, in the choir, and as Sunday School teacher. At present he is a trustee, and teacher of the woman's class named for him, the E. W. Smith Class. These women are devoted to their teacher and do not hesitate to acclaim his superiority over all other teachers.</p>

      <p>One of Settle Memorial's greatest Sunday School teachers was Mr. Alexander Hill, whose tragic death a few months ago left a vacancy that will never be filled, as well as a most disconsolate group of men whose joy it was to listen every Sunday to his wonderful expounding of the scriptures. He was a member of the first church, the church in which his mother worked so long and faithfully; consequently Methodism was in his blood. He loved his church and all it stood for. Being a well-read man and a close Bible student, he was capable of holding the interest of his class, who often said that every lesson was a real sermon. Mr. Hill was not only a teacher, he was interested in all the needs of the church and ready to answer any call. His last written words were the closing lines for his next Sunday's lesson: &ldquo;Well done, thou good and faithful servant; enter thou into the joy of thy Lord.&rdquo; How applicable for himself.</p>

      <p>Miss Vitula Jones, long a teacher in the Sunday School from the early days of Settle Chapel until her health failed and compelled her to give up. She was faithful and generous to a fault where her church was concerned, and she will be justly rewarded.</p>

      <p>The Young Adult Group, formerly the Epworth League, is a thriving organization of about thirty-five members. Its officers: Glen Lovern, president; Miss Bernice Morris, vice president; Miss Anna Ford McClanahan, secretary.</p>

      <p>The High League enrolls thirty members. Its officers: Miss Barbara Mitchell, president; Ben Creamer, vice president; Brown Thigpen, treasurer.</p>

      <div class="roster">
        <p class="rtitle"><span class="folio">27</span>The Board of Stewards, 1934&ndash;1935</p>
        <ul class="names">
          <li>D. C. Stimson <i>(Chairman, resigned)</i></li>
          <li>B. D. Ringo, <i>Chairman</i></li>
          <li>J. E. Arnold</li>
          <li>T. P. Alexander</li>
          <li>Dr. George Barr</li>
          <li>Thurman L. Barr</li>
          <li>Mrs. C. A. Beaty</li>
          <li>Mrs. H. M. Berry</li>
          <li>W. D. Chambers</li>
          <li>C. W. Cromwell</li>
          <li>W. F. Dell</li>
          <li>Dr. R. P. Foster</li>
          <li>Earl Felix</li>
          <li>W. R. Gillette</li>
          <li>Delbert C. Glenn</li>
          <li>Talmage Hocker</li>
