      <p>The church now has 1,140 members.</p>

      <p>Settle Memorial has been especially blessed in her pastors. All have been educated, bright, attractive men. Each and every one had some distinguishing quality, many of them possessing great spirituality, which made their influence great not only at that time but throughout the years. Three were noted for their brilliancy; others have been equally gifted as pastors; some have been writers. &ldquo;The Coming Presence&rdquo; (Dr. Thomas) is in the church library and has been read and enjoyed by many. &ldquo;The Twentieth Century Home Builder&rdquo; (Dr. Powell) is an interesting thesis on the home and the place of woman in the home.</p>

      <p><span class="folio">28</span>The Methodist church, starting in a log cabin, has now blossomed into a wonderful, stately building that is an honor to their God. This has been possible only because those early Methodists were men and women of strong Christian characters, zealous workers, with a great vision. They set a goal and kept their eyes toward it. They were not afraid or discouraged by misfortunes or calamities, but kept their faith and moved steadily forward. The result was beautiful Settle Memorial, surmounted with a crown, its forty-eight lights sending their beams far out in all directions. What does this magnificent building mean to us? Has it taught us to &ldquo;so live that our light so shines before men that they, seeing our good work, may glorify our Father in heaven&rdquo;? Has it helped us to lay a foundation of what is right and good and beautiful? Has it been an aid to resist temptation and keep ourselves free and unspotted from the world?</p>

      <p>Let us go forward in the spirit of those early Methodists, unafraid, in perfect trust; let us live up to our heritage. May we always, as we enter this beautiful auditorium, be able to truthfully say, &ldquo;I was glad when they said unto me, let us go into the House of the Lord.&rdquo;</p>
    </section>

